1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:10,754
Gain controls how tall echoes look on the screen. It is measured in decibels, or dB, and it never moves an echo left or right.

2
00:00:10,754 --> 00:00:19,339
The Epoch is calibrated, the probe is on the 0.300 inch step, and Gate 1 is on the first back-wall echo.

3
00:00:19,339 --> 00:00:26,748
First, bring the echo to a standard height. Press 2nd F, then GATES. That is AUTO XX percent.

4
00:00:26,748 --> 00:00:36,510
The Epoch sets the gain so the gated echo sits at 80 percent of full screen height. That gain, 42.5 dB, is our reference.

5
00:00:36,510 --> 00:00:44,703
Press dB so the knob controls gain. The delta in the gain box shows the step: 1 dB per click.

6
00:00:44,703 --> 00:00:48,975
Press the check key until the step reads 6 dB.

7
00:00:48,975 --> 00:00:55,991
Every 6 dB is a factor of two. Take away 6 dB: one click to the left.

8
00:00:55,991 --> 00:01:00,655
The echo drops from 80 to 40 percent. Half the height.

9
00:01:00,655 --> 00:01:08,848
Another 6 dB down, 12 dB in total. Now it is at 20 percent, one quarter of where it started.

10
00:01:08,848 --> 00:01:17,041
Two clicks to the right brings us back to the reference gain, and the echo is back at 80 percent.

11
00:01:17,041 --> 00:01:22,881
Now add 6 dB above the reference. The echo should double, to 160 percent.

12
00:01:22,881 --> 00:01:34,604
But the screen only goes to 100 percent, so the echo runs off the top. The amplitude box just says more than 110 percent. It is really at 160.

13
00:01:34,604 --> 00:01:44,758
An echo off the top of the screen is saturated. You cannot measure its height, so bring the gain back down before you compare echoes.

14
00:01:44,758 --> 00:01:52,167
Remember: 6 dB doubles or halves the height. 12 dB is four times. 20 dB is ten times.

15
00:01:52,167 --> 00:01:59,941
Now try it yourself. Predict each height first, then turn the knob and check your answer.
